>How would copyright/trademark/patent
>etc. work under private policentric law?

Common law version:
Someone would make a complaint against someone else in a court, claiming 
damages from violation of a copyright/trademark/patent. The judge would rule
for or against and the precedent would be set. Criteria used should be
reasonableness and 
customary use. The decision is not set in stone, since parties covered by
precedents can contract around the existing law, creating a new custom. 

This also applies to your child abuse case, etc. Someone sues someone else.
Judgement and precedent. Historical common law would (I think) have left the
you 
with no recourse, but that is a result of the historical (male-dominated?)
development of the common law, not of the technique itself.

The subtle question there is what would be considered a reasonable way to
contract around a judgement about child abuse. This also would be decided as
part of the 
process, and I need not second guess the judges of the future to choose it over
the alternatives. Perhaps a PPL with a predeclared law.
 
Then the question moves to what you should do about that PPL next door that 
practices a hellish version of 'spare the rod spoil the child?' I give up here
& admit that some people cannot tolerate each other. 
