Date: Wed, 24 Mar 93 22:17:56 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: PHIL: Identity, Transformation, Uploading (LONG)

Philosophy invades the anarcho-techno-geeks!  Chaos results!

Max More writes:

> Crucial to such a theory of personal continuity will be (a) a set
> of clear distinctions between different types of change;

Agreed; I was hoping you'd give some, but I didn't see any!

Definitions (summarized from Max's post):
  Psychological connectedness: 
    > the holding of particular direct psychological
    > connections, such as memories, intentions, dispositions.
  Psychological continuity:
    > the holding of overlapping chains of strong connectedness.
    
Here is where the pseudoprecise language becomes a bit hard to stomach --
because the important question then is exactly:  What constitutes
"strong" connectedness?  Answer (sorry to quote all this):
    
> Strong connectedness exists where *enough* direct connections 
> persist over a day.  Where we draw the line and declare there
> to be "enough" connections, and thus that identity exists,
> clearly is not precisely determined.  We can set a plausible boundary, say
> where we require the persistence over the course of a day of 50% of the
> number of connections that normally persist over that period.  We must a
> draw the line because the logic of identity requires one, but we should
> remember that what really matters is the underlying *degree* of
> connectedness.

1) If only 50% (given the unlikely premise that we could count them) 
   of my psychological connections held from one day to the next, that
   seems way way too few to imply "strong" connectedness.

2) That may seem sort of nitpicky but the point is to highlight the
   silliness of talking about a "logic of identity" when it rests on
   such dubious premises.

3) Replacing "*enough* direct connections" with "the underlying *degree*
   of connectedness" doesn't explain anything.

Despite these objections, I choose to play:

> Once the criterion for strong connectedness is decided, we
> can determine whether there has been an unbroken chain of *strong
> connectedness*, i.e., psychological continuity.

Okay, that seems reasonable enough -- but why then do you shrug off
Locke's view by saying that memory is unimportant (right after putting
it first in the list of psychological connections)?  Suppose I fix up
Locke's connectedness ideas in the following two ways:

A) Instead of "I am a continuer of a previous me if I have memories
that the previous me had", replace that with the transitive closure: "I
am a continuer of a previous me if I have memories that the previous
me had or I have memories that a different continuer of that previous
me had".

B) Perform the obvious extention to "memory" to include more than
just episodic recollection -- all lingering effects of environmental
interaction on mental function.

How does this modern Lockean view of identity hold up?

> It is not obvious
> that Max is rationally required to care about PostMax any more than he should 
> care about any other person with whom he is largely unconnected.

I find the following notation to be more useful: For convenience let's
examine a series of Max snapshots.  Max(0) wrote the article to which
I'm responding.  Max(N) is the above-mentioned PostMax.  The intermediate
numbering is chosen arbitrarily such that Max(i+1) is "strongly connected"
to Max(i).  By your above definition of psychological continuity,
such a sequence i = 0..N exists.  Since Max(i) is strongly connected
to Max(i+1), he wants Max(i+1) (and all other future Max(j)'s to which
Max(i) is strongly connected) to be "better".  Won't this sequence
lead naturally to some Max(N)?  Thus "caring" about Max(N) is perfectly
reasonable, since to Max(i) he represents a (closer) future for Max(i+1)
about which it is rational to care -- and a strongly connected Max's
future is clearly an aspect of his life to care about.  The only way that
this would NOT work is if there is some k such that Max(i+k) is not strongly
connected to Max(i) and for all j between i and i+k, Max(j) is "worse" than
Max(i).  That is, the first successor to Max(i) that is "better" is no
longer strongly connected to Max(i).  This is unlikely to occur, in my
opinion, and if it doesn't, we're OK.

Comments?

derek(0)
