Thomas,

>From what you have said, I am guessing that you have not actually  
read what Browne has to say on this topic.  My copy of the book, as  
it turns out, is in a box right now (I am preparing to move).  As I  
recall (I read the book about 15 years ago), his recommendations are  
motivated by neither taxes nor market competition.  They have to do  
with finding the freedom to do what you want, to not do what you do  
not want, and to avoid conflicts over _who decides_.  For example,  
who decides what your contribution to the business should be?  Who  
decides what portion of the business's profits are yours?  Who  
decides how to expand your business, how to modify your business  
goals, how to sell your business (and for what price), how to  
re-invest profits in your business, etc.?

By not having a board of directors, by not having even a partner, the  
authority for these decisions is clear.  


Thomas continues:

>I like Mark Miller and Eric Drexler's analogy between firms and  
>software. From that point of view, this trend in management rethinks  
>the granularity of modules in hopes of making them more reuseable.  
>Some modules will be small, some large, some specialized, others  
general in response to the problems attacked and strategies employed.

Thomas,

I believe that questions of granularity, or packaging, are  
independent of the approach that Browne advocates.  One can own, as  
an individual, a "big" specialized business, that performs large jobs  
for clients such as Boeing.  One also can own a "small" general  
business (e.g., an accounting firm) that has many clients, themselves  
big or small.  To use the software analogy, objects (firms) can be  
viewed as containers.  They present an interface (to their customers  
and suppliers/service providers).  How they are implemented is  
another matter.  Implementing them in terms of smaller objects  
(firms) is always an option.  The code in an object that orchestrates  
(to nest analogies) a collection of constituent objects is analogous  
to the person who hires many firms to constitute his business.  That  
is, he orchestrates the products and services provided by these  
sub-contractors into his business.  The notion is recursive.  The  
base case being individual contractors/consultants that do not  
sub-contract.  


Some firms (whether or not they sub-contract) may have only one  
client at a time.  This does not make employees out of the set of  
persons who comprise the firm; a firm sells a product or service.  An  
employee is a one-person firm that sells a _special_ service: doing  
whatever your client tells you (and bitching about having to).  It is  
the cozy arrangement between clients who aren't quite sure what they  
want from consultants who aren't quite sure they can deliver.  The  
employer/employee relationship is a support mechanism for the  
mutually insecure.

-Pete
