Date:     Thu, 25 Mar 93 6:58:06 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Chomsky, Israel and Extortion

If Simon Levy was referring to my post when he said that one had said that
his true colors could be discerned from where he gets published, then that's
not an accurate representation of my position.  If he wasn't, it still isn't,
but it was never meant to be.  At any rate, I feel the need to clarify myself.

Groucho Marx said that politics is the art of looking for trouble, finding
it everywhere, misdiagnosing it and mistreating it.  In this sense, I'm
something of a Marxist.  Chomsky looks for trouble, only seems to find it
in the USA's National Security State and/or its corporatist economy,
diagnoses it correctly seemingly as often as not, and reccomends the right
remedy seemingly as often as not.

Beyond that, we have to go into the different kinds of class theory.  In
the 19th century, sociology came up with two basic kinds of class theory:
the kind Marx promoted, based upon the relationshp of the individual to
the means of production, and that of Comte and Dunoyer, based upon the
relationship of the individual to the means of coercion.  The first kind
has been dominant for quite some time now, but fragmented in the 1970s.
All sorts of new variants on it have sprung up such as gender feminism,
Black Power-style race-as-class theory, and Queer theory.  None of thse
seems to be as universal in scope as marxist class theory, however, and
thus none of the proposed alternatives has completely taken its place.

The second kind of class theory, that based upon one's relationshp to the
means of coercion, is the libertarian one, as in Albert Jay Nock's
"Our Enemy, the State."  It is also, I would submit, the correct one.

Chomsky tends towards the marxist class theory.  Using this, he often
concurs with conclusions that are arrived at by application of libertarian
class theory, such as anti-imperialism, but not always, and not necessarily.
Somehow the imperialism of marxist regimes has largely escaped criticism
from Chomsky and the Left, who've concentrated on relatively minor
oppressive regimes such as Chile and South Africa that are far from perfect,
but a damn sight better than Peru and Nigeria.

Another confusing tactic that is SOP in marxist repertory is to bundle a
reasonable position together with an unreasonable one such that they are
allegedly one and the same.  For instance, the incidence of rape will be
objected to, reasonably and correctly, and alleged to have the root cause
of the ideology of patriarchy.  If you disagree with the monocausal statement
that rape is caused by patriarchy, you are then accused of being in favor
of rape.  Thus, if you disagree with the unreasonable claim, you are accused
of disagreeing with the reasonable one as well.  This tactic isn't the sole
province of the Left any longer, but that's where it arose and that's who
does it most and best.

Thus, Chomsky will often criticize the US Corporate-Security State reasonably
and correctly, and combine this with the claim (perhaps only implicitly in
some cases) that this is the inevitable result of laissez-faire and that
the only solution is to adopt his form of anarco-syndicalism.

Those adept at analyzing politics, as most of us tend to be, have little
trouble making distinctions between what we find true and false in this sort
of argument, but we (Promethean NTs, to use Myers-Briggs) are by far in the
minority amongst humanity.  We must take great care not to mislead those
that may be less logically adept.

(As Andrew Hall will be able to guess, I'm another defender of Myers-Briggs
psychological temperament theory.  Merely accusing it of being equivalent to
phrenology because it isn't "falsifiable" in some sense is scientism.  And
note that while Andrew didn't actually compare it to phrenology, phrenology
didn't arise in much of a fringe, but amongst mainstream, conventional
intellectuals, which says something about being mainstream and conventional.)

Enough of that.  On to Israel: according to Paul Johnson's "Modern Times,"
Israel is the result of the only succesful terrorist attempt to establish a
nation-state in history.  It certainy practices national socialism, if only
for the Jewish nation rather than the Aryan one.

And, lastly, extortion.  George O'Brien has argued to me that extortion will
be one of the most common forms of crime in a libertarian society.  The
trade-off is that most other forms will decrease more than enough to offset
this.  And, of course, there is economic reason to believe that it will
only exist at an equilibrium level of some sort, too: if I demand $10,000
from you or else, you may well find it easier to pay $9,000 to an extortion-
insurance agency to prevent me from carrying out my threat - in which case
I'll have to lower my bid.

