Subject: Sociobiology

Thomas D. Burns:
> My impression is that sociobiology attempts to explain behavior strictly
> in terms of genetics, downplaying cognition and culture. Anybody have a 
> further clarification?

OK, some academic administrivia:

* Many in the field (English in particular) don't use the word 
"sociobiology", prefering "behavioral ecology" or just "ethology",
but the theories are the same (those outlined by Dawkins, Wilson, et. 
al.).  I'm just using the most popular term for the field.

* Dawkins spends 10 chapters (11 in the new edition) on genetic
evolution and genetic motivations for behavior, and one chapter
on memetics/cultural evolution.

* While sociobiology accepts cultural influence, their field of
study is genetic influences and how they interact with cultural
influences.  A couple examples among many: they are tackling the 
puzzle of how a genetic disposition for homosexuality could survive in 
the gene pool, and exploring theories of predisposed motivations for 
crime (it turns out much in crime demographics follows from sociobiology
theory: the age of rape victims, the motives for murder, etc.)
Sociobiology tends to focus on the sexual and emotional, but the field 
has major implications for linguistics (Chomsky's universal grammar 
assumes a complex instinct), economics (under what conditions is the 
assumption of "rational self-interest" predictive of human motivation), 
and just about every other study of humans.

* It is fruitless IMHO to study [ir]rationality, sexuality, and
human motivation in general without a good understanding of human 
genetic predispositions, how they influence ourselves and our culture.
The biggest single first step in self-transformation is understading 
who we are now, where our most basic emotions and motivations come 
from, culturally and genetically.  The best book I have seen on this, 
the basic textbook that should be in every memetic engineer's toolbox, 
is _The Evolution of Human Sexuality_ by Donald Symons.  This goes 
into the anthropological literature (the study of human culture) in 
detail, describing where genetic influence plays a role, and how 
the predictions of socbiology theory are reflected in human culture.
Can't recommend it enough.

* Lumsden & Wilson have started to develop a theory of cultural/genetic
co-evolution, but they haven't gotten very far.  There are too many
scholars polarized into "only culture is important" and "only genes
are important" camps, when in fact just about every human characteristic
of interest is influenced by both, and not enough scholars from either
camp are willing to work together to deepen our understanding of humanity.
This is further exacerbated by the difficulty many otherwise
intelligent people have understanding the basic theory of natural
selection, but that's another story.   The political agitation
to try to shut down sociobiology, perceived as a major threat to
political correctness & established neo-Saint-Simonian theories
in sociology etc., is yet a third story....


